# DIFC arbitration costs framework — PD 4/2017 + RDC 38.40 + 80% practice convention

Sources (each pinned in `difc_practice_direction_4_2017_source.yaml`):
  - DIFC Courts Practice Direction No. 4 of 2017 — Interest on Judgments.
  - Rules of the DIFC Courts (RDC) 38.40 — payment timeline.
  - DIFC arbitration costs practice convention (80% discount) — drawn
    from the DIFC Courts' published costs orders, not a single rule.

ATTRIBUTION (per RULES-ATTRIBUTION.md): © DIFC Courts. Reproduced
under fair-dealing for research, with attribution, in a non-commercial
academic working paper.

NOTE: this module composes three sources. The original file labelled
itself "Practice Direction No. 4 of 2017 — Arbitration costs framework",
but PD 4/2017 is in fact titled "Interest on Judgments" and supplies
only the interest-on-late-payment limb (9% pa). The 14-day payment gate
comes from RDC 38.40, and the 80% receiving-party discount is a
practice convention applied across DIFC-seated arbitration costs
orders. The encoding's logic and tests are unchanged; only the citation
structure is corrected here.

## PD 4/2017 — Interest on Judgments

DIFC Practice Direction No. 4 of 2017 supersedes the two earlier 2009
practice directions on the same subject and is to be read alongside
DIFC Law No. 10 of 2004 and the RDC (in particular RDC 45.26).

VERBATIM QUOTES from the DIFC Courts website rendering (the official
website summarises the Practice Direction; the inner quoted phrases
below are the verbatim operative language):

  1. Citation clause: "This Practice Direction will come into effect
     on the date of signature. It may be cited as Practice Direction
     4 of 2017 – Interest on Judgments."

  2. Pre-effective-date judgments: such judgments carry interest
     "from the date the judgment is entered, at the rate of 1% over
     the Emirates Interbank Offer Rate (\"EIBOR\")", or at a judge-
     prescribed rate, by reference to the three-month EIBOR rate at
     the date of the judgment.

  3. Post-effective-date judgments: such judgments carry "simple
     interest, from the date the judgment is entered, at the rate of
     9%" or such other rate as the judge may prescribe.

  4. Signature: Chief Justice of the DIFC Courts, dated 20 November
     2017.

[VERIFY against the official PDF: the DIFC Courts website's rendered
HTML page for PD 4/2017 paraphrases the surrounding text; only the
inner quoted phrases above are verbatim operative language. The full
practice direction PDF, if separately published, would be the
authoritative source for citation.]

Structurally the Practice Direction encodes two interest regimes
gated by the effective date:

  Regime A (pre/on 20 November 2017): rate = EIBOR_3mo + 1%
  Regime B (post 20 November 2017):   rate = 9% pa simple
  Both regimes: judge may prescribe a different rate.

## RDC 38.40 — Time for complying with an order for costs (verbatim)

Reproduced verbatim from the Rules of the DIFC Courts:

38.40 A party must comply with an order for the payment of costs within
14 days of:

  (1) the date of the judgment or order if it states the amount of
      those costs;

  (2) if the amount of those costs (or part of them) is decided later
      in accordance with Part 40, the date of the certificate which
      states the amount; or

  (3) in either case, such later date as the Court may specify.

## 80% receiving-party discount — DIFC arbitration costs practice

The 80% figure is a practice convention rather than a published rule.
Across DIFC-seated arbitration costs orders the receiving party is
generally awarded 80% of its reasonable costs, the 20% discount
reflecting the restricted character of arbitration-support work and
the limited need for full forensic engagement at the arbitration-court
interface. This convention is captured here as a parameter so it can
be overridden where a costs order departs from it.

## Composition of the three sources

The encoding combines the three sources as follows:
  - principal = reasonable_costs × 0.80          (practice convention)
  - in_breach iff days_paid_after_order > 14     (RDC 38.40)
  - interest = principal × 0.09 × accrued/365    (PD 4/2017)
  - accrued = days_paid_after_order - 14         (RDC 38.40 deadline)

This composition is so consistent across DIFC arbitration costs orders
that the corpus has converged on a near-formula — captured here as a
single deterministic predicate.

## Encoding

The interest rate is parametric — `simple_interest_rate` accepts either
PD 4/2017 regime depending on when the judgment was entered:

  - For judgments entered AFTER 20 November 2017: pass 0.09 (the
    post-effective-date 9% pa default).
  - For judgments entered ON OR BEFORE 20 November 2017: pass
    `(EIBOR_3mo + 0.01)` evaluated at the date of the judgment.
  - For judgments where the judge prescribed a different rate
    (either regime): pass the prescribed rate.

The encoding does not select between the two regimes itself — that
selection is a date arithmetic that the caller performs against the
judgment date and PD 4/2017's effective date (20 November 2017).

```catala
declaration structure ArbitrationCostsClaim:
  data reasonable_costs_aed content decimal
  data discount_rate content decimal           ## 0.80 by convention
  data deadline_days content decimal           ## 14.0 by convention
  data days_paid_after_order content decimal
  data simple_interest_rate content decimal    ## 0.09 post-2017-11-20;
                                                ## EIBOR_3mo+0.01 if pre.

declaration structure ArbitrationCostsAward:
  data principal_aed content decimal
  data deadline_days content decimal
  data in_breach content boolean
  data days_accrued content decimal
  data interest_aed content decimal
  data total_owed_aed content decimal

declaration scope OutstandingArbitrationCosts:
  input claim content ArbitrationCostsClaim
  output award content ArbitrationCostsAward

scope OutstandingArbitrationCosts:
  definition award equals
    let principal equals claim.reasonable_costs_aed * claim.discount_rate in
    ## "In breach" iff payment landed past the deadline.
    let in_breach equals claim.days_paid_after_order > claim.deadline_days in
    let accrued equals
      if in_breach then claim.days_paid_after_order - claim.deadline_days
      else 0.00
    in
    let interest equals
      principal * claim.simple_interest_rate * (accrued / 365.00)
    in
    ArbitrationCostsAward {
      -- principal_aed: principal
      -- deadline_days: claim.deadline_days
      -- in_breach: in_breach
      -- days_accrued: accrued
      -- interest_aed: interest
      -- total_owed_aed: principal + interest
    }
```

## Used in

trace-02 — ARB 008/2026 *Oberlin v Ovidiu* (RDC 38.40 + PD 4/2017). Five
scenarios reproduce: on-time, at-deadline, 1 / 61 / 92 days late.

## Tests

```catala
#[test] declaration scope TestPD4_PaidOnTime:
  composite scope OutstandingArbitrationCosts
  output award content ArbitrationCostsAward

scope TestPD4_PaidOnTime:
  definition award equals composite.award
  definition composite.claim equals
    ArbitrationCostsClaim {
      -- reasonable_costs_aed: 95982.26
      -- discount_rate: 0.80
      -- deadline_days: 14.0
      -- days_paid_after_order: 14.0
      -- simple_interest_rate: 0.09
    }
  ## Paid on the 14th day = on the deadline = not in breach.
  assertion award.in_breach = false
  assertion award.interest_aed = 0.00

#[test] declaration scope TestPD4_Unpaid61Days:
  composite scope OutstandingArbitrationCosts
  output award content ArbitrationCostsAward

scope TestPD4_Unpaid61Days:
  definition award equals composite.award
  ## Receiver remained unpaid 61 days past the order — 47 days past
  ## the deadline.
  definition composite.claim equals
    ArbitrationCostsClaim {
      -- reasonable_costs_aed: 95982.26
      -- discount_rate: 0.80
      -- deadline_days: 14.0
      -- days_paid_after_order: 61.0
      -- simple_interest_rate: 0.09
    }
  assertion award.in_breach = true

#[test] declaration scope TestPD4_EIBORPreEffectiveDateRegime:
  composite scope OutstandingArbitrationCosts
  output award content ArbitrationCostsAward

scope TestPD4_EIBORPreEffectiveDateRegime:
  definition award equals composite.award
  ## Hypothetical: a judgment entered before 20 November 2017 with
  ## the EIBOR pre-effective-date regime active. Caller computes
  ## (EIBOR_3mo + 0.01) at the judgment date and passes it as
  ## simple_interest_rate. Here we use a representative figure of
  ## EIBOR_3mo = 2.5% → effective rate 3.5%. The encoding's
  ## arithmetic is unchanged; only the rate input differs.
  definition composite.claim equals
    ArbitrationCostsClaim {
      -- reasonable_costs_aed: 95982.26
      -- discount_rate: 0.80
      -- deadline_days: 14.0
      -- days_paid_after_order: 61.0
      -- simple_interest_rate: 0.035
    }
  assertion award.in_breach = true
```
